WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.041 --> 00:00:09.794
In 1867 a young man named John Muir walked a thousand miles across the United States.

2
00:00:09.795 --> 00:00:16.015
Muir had already had many outdoor adventures in his life, but this journey was special because it almost didn't happen.

3
00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:21.077
Six months earlier, a factory accident had temporarily blinded Muir, changing him forever.

4
00:00:21.078 --> 00:00:28.235
The despair he felt at the thought of never again seeing the wilderness was so strong that he vowed to spend the rest of his life studying nature.

5
00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:36.062
He later also decided to share his passion by starting a career as a writer—in order to "entice people to look at nature's loveliness."

6
00:00:36.063 --> 00:00:44.276
Over the years John Muir's explorations and writings changed people's views of wilderness and inspired many to take up the cause of preservation.

7
00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:53.655
John Muir was born on April 21, 1838, in Dunbar, Scotland. One of eight siblings, he spent hours exploring the outdoors as a young boy.

8
00:00:53.656 --> 00:00:59.537
Muir attended school until he was 11, when his family moved to America and settled on a farm in Wisconsin.

9
00:00:59.538 --> 00:01:05.856
Muir's strict, religious father put his children to work in the fields from dawn to dusk; there was no time for school.

10
00:01:05.857 --> 00:01:10.429
But Muir was curious about the world around him and began teaching himself with books.

11
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:16.247
By the age of 15, he had developed a keen interest in mechanical devices and hoped to become an inventor.

12
00:01:16.248 --> 00:01:23.126
He received permission from his father to rise five hours before dawn each morning so he could read books and tinker with machinery.

13
00:01:23.127 --> 00:01:29.514
To make sure sleep didn't steal this precious time, he invented an alarm clock to tip his bed and knock him onto the floor.

14
00:01:29.515 --> 00:01:35.449
Then he would hurry down to the cellar where, working by candlelight, he crafted clocks and other gadgets.

15
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:40.012
When he was 22 years old, Muir displayed his inventions at the state fair.

16
00:01:40.013 --> 00:01:47.684
His work was praised for its ingenuity, which encouraged Muir to continue his education. He decided to study at the University of Wisconsin.

17
00:01:47.685 --> 00:01:50.560
Muir pursued his interest in invention in college.

18
00:01:50.561 --> 00:01:58.329
In fact, he kept up with his coursework by building a desk that could open up a book for a set time and then automatically replace it with another.

19
00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.163
But Muir's interest in the natural world was growing.

20
00:02:01.164 --> 00:02:09.486
In 1864 he left college to visit Canada, where he wandered in the wilderness, collecting botanical specimens and sketching in a notebook.

21
00:02:09.487 --> 00:02:16.913
To support himself, Muir worked at a Canadian sawmill and broom-making factory, where his mechanical skills proved invaluable.

22
00:02:16.914 --> 00:02:24.970
Muir left Canada in 1866, returning to the United States to accept a job at an Indiana factory and to study the local landscape.

23
00:02:24.971 --> 00:02:34.074
Disaster struck on March 6, 1867; a tool slipped from Muir's hand and pierced his right eye, causing him to go blind in both eyes.

24
00:02:34.075 --> 00:02:38.809
The threat of permanent blindness allowed Muir to see what was truly important to him.

25
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:49.000
When Muir's vision returned, he dedicated himself to the study of nature, writing, "I bade adieu to mechanical inventions, determined to devote the rest of my life to the inventions of God."

26
00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:57.343
As soon as his eyesight returned, Muir began his thousand-mile walk, traveling from Kentucky to Florida and observing plant life along the way.

27
00:02:57.344 --> 00:03:00.768
He then took a boat to Cuba, where he spent a month in the jungle.

28
00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:07.621
Muir hoped to continue his trip with a raft ride down the Amazon River, but he could not find a boat sailing to South America.

29
00:03:07.622 --> 00:03:15.201
Seeing cheap fares to California, he decided to visit San Francisco instead. This decision would change the rest of his life.

30
00:03:15.202 --> 00:03:23.206
Within days of his arrival, he started walking to Yosemite Valley, in the Sierra Nevada, a vast mountain range in California and Nevada.

31
00:03:23.207 --> 00:03:30.976
There a rancher hired Muir to herd sheep, offering Muir an extraordinary opportunity to study Yosemite and the mountains around it.

32
00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:37.315
He was awed by the beauty and serenity of Yosemite and the Sierra Nevada, a place he called the "Range of Light."

33
00:03:37.316 --> 00:03:42.193
Muir worked a variety of jobs but spent as much time as he could studying the mountain range.

34
00:03:42.194 --> 00:03:47.200
As word of Muir's knowledge spread, visitors began to seek him out for guided tours.

35
00:03:47.201 --> 00:03:55.290
By 1871 Muir had begun to write about the natural wonders of the landscape. He also shared his views on the damage caused by people.

36
00:03:55.291 --> 00:04:02.838
Tourists left garbage scattered about. Farmers' sheep devoured everything before them—for which he called them "hooved locusts."

37
00:04:02.839 --> 00:04:09.266
In 1873 Muir left the wilderness of the valley to enlist support for its preservation through his writing.

38
00:04:09.267 --> 00:04:15.819
His articles became popular among both the common people of California and influential figures across the country.

39
00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:23.661
Although Muir had many friends in California and many more with whom he exchanged letters, his long walks were usually done alone, and he was lonely.

40
00:04:23.662 --> 00:04:30.208
In 1880 Muir finally found love and married, settling down in California to run his father-in-law's ranch.

41
00:04:30.209 --> 00:04:39.527
He spent the winters on the ranch—"lost and choked in agricultural needs," as he wrote—but he continued to travel during the rest of the year and write about his adventures in the wild.

42
00:04:39.528 --> 00:04:46.129
He especially loved to tell the story of a trek across an Alaskan glacier on which he was accompanied by a dog named Stickeen.

43
00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:53.240
Together Muir and the brave dog crossed a treacherous sliver of ice—a long, narrow bridge hundreds of feet in the air.

44
00:04:53.241 --> 00:05:02.128
Throughout the next decade, Muir continued to call for preservation of the Yosemite area, saying that wilderness is necessary for people's physical and spiritual health.

45
00:05:02.129 --> 00:05:08.818
He encouraged city dwellers to experience the wonders of nature, writing, "Come to the woods, for here is rest."

46
00:05:08.819 --> 00:05:18.494
The United States Congress finally declared the Yosemite region a national park in 1890, but Muir pressed for further expansion of the federal national parks system.

47
00:05:18.495 --> 00:05:25.508
In 1903, when President Theodore Roosevelt visited Yosemite Valley, he camped alone with Muir for several days.

48
00:05:25.509 --> 00:05:29.959
The two men became lifelong friends, and Muir gained a powerful ally.

49
00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:38.855
During his presidency, Roosevelt established five national parks and protected other sites, such as the Grand Canyon, by declaring them national monuments.

50
00:05:38.856 --> 00:05:45.165
The impact Muir had on the president is one reason he is now considered the father of America's national parks.

51
00:05:45.166 --> 00:05:54.792
Muir continued to travel and advocate for preservation until his seventies, and he never regretted his choice to abandon a career as an inventor to follow his love for the outdoors.

52
00:05:54.793 --> 00:06:04.827
He died on December 24, 1914. During his lifetime he had over three hundred articles and ten books published, and his influence extends to the modern day.

53
00:06:04.828 --> 00:06:12.275
He was a founder of the Sierra Club, an organization still committed to helping people "explore, enjoy, and protect the planet."

54
00:06:12.276 --> 00:06:19.313
Many people are reminded of his contributions as they walk in the Muir Woods, a grove of thousand-year-old trees in California.

55
00:06:19.314 --> 00:06:29.436
This grove might have been destroyed if Muir had not helped people recognize "that wildness is necessity; that mountain parks and reservations are . . . fountains of life."

56
00:06:29.437 --> 00:06:38.308
John Muir inspired people everywhere to embrace the wonders of nature, and his legacy survives in today's efforts to preserve wilderness around the world.
